<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as PDPA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's Democratic Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> was a Marxist-Leninist party founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1965 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a> and others. Split into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> factions, it seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of April 1978 and ruled the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan with Soviet backing. Renamed the Watan Party in 1990, it lost power when the mujahideen took Kabul in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan.jpg" alt="People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Falerístico · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_People%27s_Democratic_Party_of_Afghanistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was formed at a unity congress of factions of the Socialist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> on 1 January 1965. Twenty-seven men met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a>'s house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and elected him Secretary General, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babrak-karmal">Babrak Karmal</a> as Deputy Secretary General. Its secret constitution described its ideology as the practical experience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>, but in public it used labels such as national democratic and socialist. Four members won seats in the 1965 parliamentary election, and the party was known for its ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1967 the party split into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalq">Khalq</a> faction of Taraki, drawing mostly on rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a> faction of Karmal, drawing on urban supporters. Soviet mediation reunited them in 1977. The party helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a> overthrow King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-zahir-shah">Mohammad Zahir Shah</a> in 1973, but relations with him worsened, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of 1978 the party seized power, with Taraki as prime minister.</p><p>The government pursued rapid reform: separating mosque and state, a literacy campaign, land reform and the emancipation of women. It also launched a repression that killed thousands, mostly at Pul-e-Charkhi prison, where estimates of those executed between April 1978 and December 1979 run as high as 27,000. Olivier Roy estimated that between 50,000 and 100,000 people disappeared in the Taraki–Amin period. In December 1979 Soviet special forces killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a> at the Tajbeg Palace, and Karmal became leader as the Soviet–Afghan War began. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-najibullah">Mohammad Najibullah</a> replaced Karmal as general secretary in May 1986. After the Soviet withdrawal in 1989, Najibullah renamed the party the Homeland Party in June 1990 and dropped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>. Mujahideen took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1992 and banned the party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party was organised on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>, usually of seven to nine members, as its main executive body between sessions of the congress and the Central Committee. From 1982 it spread into the countryside, though it was organised in only about 2,000 of an estimated 25,000 villages. Its successors included the Watan Party, formed in 1997, and the National United Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, registered in 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</b> seized power in the Saur Revolution of April 1978, killing President Daoud Khan of the Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978).</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-afghanistan">Democratic Republic of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>On 27 April 1978 army officers loyal to the <b>People's Democratic Party</b> killed Daoud and his family in the Saur Revolution, establishing the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">Republic of Afghanistan (1973-1978)</a></li></ul></section>
